<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Armistice of Cassibile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3 September <a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/september-3">On this day: 3 September</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice</a> of Cassibile was the armistice between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, signed secretly on 3 September 1943 at an Allied camp near Cassibile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and announced on 8 September. Italy surrendered unconditionally, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at once occupied the centre and north of the country and disarmed much of the Italian army. The king and the Badoglio government fled south, and Italy remained divided until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/armistice-of-cassibile.jpg" alt="Armistice of Cassibile" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Castellano_signs_the_armistice_on_behalf_of_Badoglio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The armistice with the Allies, signed at <b>Cassibile</b> on 3 September and announced on 8 September 1943, was followed by the flight of the king and government from Rome to Brindisi, leaving the army without orders as Germany occupied central and northern Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1943 the Kingdom of Italy signed the <b>Armistice of Cassibile</b> and, after the German Operation Achse, joined the Allies; in the next two years Felice Cascione wrote Italian lyrics to the tune, and his &quot;Fischia il vento&quot; (The Wind Blows) became one of the best-known songs of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/katyusha">Katyusha</a></li><li><blockquote>Italian and Allied officers signed the <b>Armistice of Cassibile</b> in Sicily, the same day British troops landed in Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
